(582143, 58473, 8125, '<td valign="top">The b/w really works well here.  Couldn''t imagine it color.  Well done and congrats on the blue.  Excellent work  :-)</td>', 'The b/w really works well here.  Couldn''t imagine it color.  Well done and congrats on the blue.  Excellent work  :-)', '2004-02-16 16:58:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(582168, 59298, 9411, '<td valign="top">I gave your shot a 5.<br/> <br/>To me the major draw back is the tilt. Yes, many cookbooks have tilted images, but this feels as if the pie will slide off the table. It is not an enhancement to the image.<br/> <br/>The pie itself is not that attractive. Too much crumb topping not enough apples and the ice cream should have a little melt to it so it looks real. There are too many crumbs on the plate. <br/> <br/>If your going to tilt the entire scene it should be done so it shows more of the pie. More of a looking down slightly from an angle. This is shot straight on which makes the tilt even more noticeable. Also changing your angle should help eliminate what looks like blur on the top of the icecream. All of the food should be in focus, or only a very specific area if you are going for extreme shortened DOF.<br/> <br/>Bring the entire image closer to the edge and closer to the viewer.<br/>The apples in the background are good, no need for the out of focus cinnamon in the foreground, it''s just a distraction on the checked tablecloth.<br/> <br/>Food styling and photography may be one of the hardest techniques to perfect.</td>', 'I gave your shot a 5. To me the major draw back is the tilt. Yes, many cookbooks have tilted images, but this feels as if the pie will slide off the table. It is not an enhancement to the image. The pie itself is not that attractive. Too much crumb topping not enough apples and the ice cream should have a little melt to it so it looks real. There are too many crumbs on the plate.  If your going to tilt the entire scene it should be done so it shows more of the pie. More of a looking down slightly from an angle. This is shot straight on which makes the tilt even more noticeable. Also changing your angle should help eliminate what looks like blur on the top of the icecream. All of the food should be in focus, or only a very specific area if you are going for extreme shortened DOF. Bring the entire image closer to the edge and closer to the viewer.The apples in the background are good, no need for the out of focus cinnamon in the foreground, it''s just a distraction on the checked tablecloth. Food styling and photography may be one of the hardest techniques to perfect.', '2004-02-16 17:10:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(582175, 58941, 9411, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the critique club.<br/>THis is a really fine image. Not too much to critique here!<br/> <br/>Actually the more I look at this, I have no suggestions for improvement.<br/> <br/>Congrats on 4th place.</td>', 'Greetings from the critique club.THis is a really fine image. Not too much to critique here! Actually the more I look at this, I have no suggestions for improvement. Congrats on 4th place.', '2004-02-16 17:14:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(582217, 58332, 2553, '<td valign="top">I did rate this fairly low for several reasons:<br/>1. The compass is not in focus (I thought it was included as an intended subject), has a distracting cast shadow, and is partially cropped with the very dark area of the compass case leading the eye right out of the frame.<br/>2. A topographical map means nothing to me, other than that I know what one is, and the way I "read" your photograph was that the compass was the subject and you were using the map as sort of background reference material to show that you would use a compass to find your way through the Pecos Wilderness.<br/>3. The extremely shallow dof at the foreground made my eyes feel like they were going blurry!<br/> <br/>I really do like your concept, but would like to have seen it shot at a slightly different angle perhaps.<br/> <br/></td>', 'I did rate this fairly low for several reasons:1. The compass is not in focus (I thought it was included as an intended subject), has a distracting cast shadow, and is partially cropped with the very dark area of the compass case leading the eye right out of the frame.2. A topographical map means nothing to me, other than that I know what one is, and the way I "read" your photograph was that the compass was the subject and you were using the map as sort of background reference material to show that you would use a compass to find your way through the Pecos Wilderness.3. The extremely shallow dof at the foreground made my eyes feel like they were going blurry! I really do like your concept, but would like to have seen it shot at a slightly different angle perhaps. ', '2004-02-16 17:34:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(582383, 59022, 10818, '<td valign="top">I really thought this would have done better.  I thought it was a wonderful photo.</td>', 'I really thought this would have done better.  I thought it was a wonderful photo.', '2004-02-16 19:05:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(582708, 59011, 15540, '<td valign="top">Thanks to everyone and their comments. I will take the suggestions and use them. The DOF on the valves is the most commented item and I''m going to try the shot again with the valves in focus. The hard part about that though, because I had tried a little bit of that already, the Melophone is so long (longer than a trumpet, the DOF was hard to acquire with the notes out of focus and to even tell what kind of instrument it was.<br/> <br/>I was trying to show the uniqueness of the instrument, rather than the common valves seen on most instruments. I''ll have to try another angle or something.<br/> <br/>Thanks again for the comments. They are greatly appreciated.</td>', 'Thanks to everyone and their comments. I will take the suggestions and use them. The DOF on the valves is the most commented item and I''m going to try the shot again with the valves in focus. The hard part about that though, because I had tried a little bit of that already, the Melophone is so long (longer than a trumpet, the DOF was hard to acquire with the notes out of focus and to even tell what kind of instrument it was. I was trying to show the uniqueness of the instrument, rather than the common valves seen on most instruments. I''ll have to try another angle or something. Thanks again for the comments. They are greatly appreciated.', '2004-02-16 22:50:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(582737, 58473, 714, '<td valign="top">Incredible!  I love the frost on the tips of the leaf.</td>', 'Incredible!  I love the frost on the tips of the leaf.', '2004-02-16 23:37:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(582795, 58473, 6870, '<td valign="top">Thanks again, everyone, for the comments! Just for the record, I did not glue the hoar frost on! At the point of taking this shot, the temperature had not risen over 5 degrees in over three weeks. It was chilly, but makes for interesting frosty images.</td>', 'Thanks again, everyone, for the comments! Just for the record, I did not glue the hoar frost on! At the point of taking this shot, the temperature had not risen over 5 degrees in over three weeks. It was chilly, but makes for interesting frosty images.', '2004-02-17 00:31:51.000000', NULL, 0),
