(591389, 59158, 5044, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club:<br/> <br/>This is my first critique, so i''ll try to do my best. I hope I can be of some help.<br/> <br/>Composition: I like the way you have agressively cropped this image. It works very well, much better than if you had shown the whole face. I especially like the partial nose. Only a little thing here that bothers me: that pink area in upper RH corner throws off the BG. <br/> <br/>Subject: I think it is a very creative photo and subject matter is very interresting. The eyelashes in the magnifying glass are especially great.<br/> <br/>Challenge: Meets the challenge topic well. Creative way to get DOF. I don''t feel that the DOF needs to be more pronounced to achieve what the topic was requiring. Your main subject stands out very well from the BG.<br/> <br/>Technical: Colours are OK, but could perhaps to be a little more intense. Focus on the main eye is very good, overall quality of the image is good. Good detail in the eye. One main thing bothers me here, mainly the reflections in the glass. Perhaps not having the bright light source behind the camera would have eliminated this.  <br/> <br/>Border: I''m not sure I like this strong of a border. I like the outer black, but probably would have gone with much less white inner border, 1-2 pixels perhaps.<br/> <br/>Overall: I think you managed to find an interresting subject and make an original photo to enter in the challenge. It works for me. I''d be very curious to see how this photo would look in BW. I think there is lots to work with here (lots of texture). <br/> <br/>Good work!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club: This is my first critique, so i''ll try to do my best. I hope I can be of some help. Composition: I like the way you have agressively cropped this image. It works very well, much better than if you had shown the whole face. I especially like the partial nose. Only a little thing here that bothers me: that pink area in upper RH corner throws off the BG.  Subject: I think it is a very creative photo and subject matter is very interresting. The eyelashes in the magnifying glass are especially great. Challenge: Meets the challenge topic well. Creative way to get DOF. I don''t feel that the DOF needs to be more pronounced to achieve what the topic was requiring. Your main subject stands out very well from the BG. Technical: Colours are OK, but could perhaps to be a little more intense. Focus on the main eye is very good, overall quality of the image is good. Good detail in the eye. One main thing bothers me here, mainly the reflections in the glass. Perhaps not having the bright light source behind the camera would have eliminated this.   Border: I''m not sure I like this strong of a border. I like the outer black, but probably would have gone with much less white inner border, 1-2 pixels perhaps. Overall: I think you managed to find an interresting subject and make an original photo to enter in the challenge. It works for me. I''d be very curious to see how this photo would look in BW. I think there is lots to work with here (lots of texture).  Good work!', '2004-02-20 22:10:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(591948, 59132, 11967, '<td valign="top">It''s beautiful Shelley!</td>', 'It''s beautiful Shelley!', '2004-02-21 08:07:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(592018, 58784, 714, '<td valign="top">Well deserved ribbon! Congrats</td>', 'Well deserved ribbon! Congrats', '2004-02-21 10:07:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(592262, 59261, 14012, '<td valign="top">**Critique Club**<br/> <br/>Greetings Bob!<br/> <br/><b>Positives:</b> <br/>The lighting for this shot, IMHO, is about perfect. None of the white appears to be blown out, at all. The red and black for the backgrounds work very nice here as well. The shallow DOF is very well done as well.<br/> <br/><b>Possible Improvements:</b> <br/>From a technical perspective, the shot is right one. The only area of possible improvement is in the composition. Maybe remove the sugar dish on the left and slide the tea pot to the left so it is more on the 1/3 line and come at it from a slightly different angle.<br/> <br/>Overall, technically a very well done photograph.<br/> <br/>-Chris<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2004-02-21 16:37:26.</i></td>', '**Critique Club** Greetings Bob! Positives: The lighting for this shot, IMHO, is about perfect. None of the white appears to be blown out, at all. The red and black for the backgrounds work very nice here as well. The shallow DOF is very well done as well. Possible Improvements: From a technical perspective, the shot is right one. The only area of possible improvement is in the composition. Maybe remove the sugar dish on the left and slide the tea pot to the left so it is more on the 1/3 line and come at it from a slightly different angle. Overall, technically a very well done photograph. -Chris', '2004-02-21 16:36:55.000000', '2004-02-21 16:37:26.000000', 0),
(592588, 59022, 10402, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club!</b> <br/> <br/><b>The message</b> <br/>This is a great photo of a beautiful and vivacious woman. The outdoor setting suits her well. It doesn''t quite catch the fabled "decisive moment"; her smile seems slightly forced. This gives it a bit of a "snapshotish" feeling--a photo that is well appreciated by those that know her, but doesn''t make a real connection to strangers.<br/> <br/><b>Creative choices</b> <br/>The "Golden Hour" lighting gives a great warm color to the subject. It also casts strong shadows that show the form of her face. There is enough ambient light (or perhaps you used a reflector?) to prevent the shadows from being too black. The point of view is complementary and works well here. Sharpness is just right. The landscape format is unusual for a portrait, but works well here to supply context and gives the image a more relaxed feeling.<br/> <br/><b>Technical aspects</b> <br/>This image is technically perfect. Focus, exposure, color balance, etc. are all just right.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  The message This is a great photo of a beautiful and vivacious woman. The outdoor setting suits her well. It doesn''t quite catch the fabled "decisive moment"; her smile seems slightly forced. This gives it a bit of a "snapshotish" feeling--a photo that is well appreciated by those that know her, but doesn''t make a real connection to strangers. Creative choices The "Golden Hour" lighting gives a great warm color to the subject. It also casts strong shadows that show the form of her face. There is enough ambient light (or perhaps you used a reflector?) to prevent the shadows from being too black. The point of view is complementary and works well here. Sharpness is just right. The landscape format is unusual for a portrait, but works well here to supply context and gives the image a more relaxed feeling. Technical aspects This image is technically perfect. Focus, exposure, color balance, etc. are all just right.', '2004-02-21 22:09:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(593158, 58302, 17387, '<td valign="top">Incredibly beautiful portrait.   I love your art.</td>', 'Incredibly beautiful portrait.   I love your art.', '2004-02-22 11:11:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(593250, 58784, 16128, '<td valign="top">Congrats on the ribbon.<br/> <br/>How did you manage to get the water droplets to stick to the glass? I''ve been attempting this kind of shot for a while and can''t seem to get it.</td>', 'Congrats on the ribbon. How did you manage to get the water droplets to stick to the glass? I''ve been attempting this kind of shot for a while and can''t seem to get it.', '2004-02-22 13:20:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(593443, 58892, 14012, '<td valign="top">**Critique Club**<br/> <br/>Hello Neil!<br/> <br/>This is a cute capture. You did a good job getting the squirrel and feeder in focus. The thing that I see where you could have improved are on the "halo" around the squirrel as some mentioned below. Because of this, it make it quite obvious that the DOF blur was done in post. Also, I think if you would not have blurred the background so much, it would have looked more natural, thus, improving the quality of the photo.<br/> <br/>Good luck on your future entries.<br/> <br/>Regards,<br/>Chris</td>', '**Critique Club** Hello Neil! This is a cute capture. You did a good job getting the squirrel and feeder in focus. The thing that I see where you could have improved are on the "halo" around the squirrel as some mentioned below. Because of this, it make it quite obvious that the DOF blur was done in post. Also, I think if you would not have blurred the background so much, it would have looked more natural, thus, improving the quality of the photo. Good luck on your future entries. Regards,Chris', '2004-02-22 19:40:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(593533, 59302, 772, '<td valign="top">Dave, I got this image as a CC image, but I think I have already commented on it with my original thoughts.  I don''t think there is much that I can really add.<br/> <br/>Reading your comments, I think it would possibly have been better serving to you if you had worked with a subject/setting that allowed you to create your shallow depth of field more with the camera.  <br/> <br/>Cheers :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Dave, I got this image as a CC image, but I think I have already commented on it with my original thoughts.  I don''t think there is much that I can really add. Reading your comments, I think it would possibly have been better serving to you if you had worked with a subject/setting that allowed you to create your shallow depth of field more with the camera.   Cheers :) John Setzler', '2004-02-22 20:43:41.000000', NULL, 0),
